From 03d92d1a2a5936185af8f6b2c17959920bdfd54b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <c@x>
Date: Fri, 27 Nov 2026 03:10:44 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] MLWeb Phase 6.1c: for over a range without building it

A for loop over a:b or a:s:b (also in parentheses) now computes one value per
iteration. Bounds are still evaluated once, in the same order. Behaviour change:
ranges too large to allocate (1:Inf, 1:1e12) used to throw an internal
allocation error and now loop until break, as in MATLAB. A loop without a break
over such a range runs until Stop or the time limit.
num() builds its one-element buffer with new Float64Array(1), which is cheaper
than Float64Array.of; it is the per-iteration cost of every loop variable.

Profile before this patch (bench.ts, bundled, node --cpu-prof, self time):
ScalarEval.run 14.6%, readPixel 12.3%, GC 9.8%, num 9.0% (8.5% of the total
called from the for loop), exec 7.5%, assignTo 7.1%.

512x512 medians, 9 alternating runs, Node 22 (noise about 15%):
  L7 brightness 337 -> 302 ms, L6 zoom 186 -> 185, L8 rotate 236 -> 218,
  L8 slicing 80 -> 62, histeq+imhist 36 -> 37.

6.1d (variable slots) measured and not implemented: 0.5-2.1 million lookups per
512x512 loop case; even if a lookup went from ~6.5 ns to 0 the gain is 3-5%.
The whole scalar evaluator is 16.6% of the profile, which bounds what compiling
expressions to closures could save.

